      "text": "To find the value of $\\cos 135^\\circ$, we can use the unit circle and the properties of the cosine function.\n\nFirst, we note that $135^\\circ$ is an angle in the second quadrant, where the cosine function is negative. We can find the reference angle for $135^\\circ$ by subtracting $90^\\circ$ from it, which gives us $45^\\circ$.\n\nNext, we can use the fact that $\\cos (180^\\circ - \\theta) = -\\cos \\theta$. In this case, $\\theta = 45^\\circ$, so we have:\n\n$$\\cos 135^\\circ = \\cos (180^\\circ - 45^\\circ) = -\\cos 45^\\circ.$$\n\nSince $\\cos 45^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$, we have:\n\n$$\\cos 135^\\circ = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$$\n\nRationalizing the denominator, we get:\n\n$$\\cos 135^\\circ = \\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}.$$",
